    "case_id": "exploration-local-stress",
    "focal_pair": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 8 USER]\ne177\tI want to pick her.\ne178\tI know that's the right answer.\ne179\tIt just feels wrong that he just gets to keep being online and doing this to other girls.\ne180\tTherapist search — where do I even start?\ne181\tI want someone who actually does this, not a general family therapist who will be out of their depth.\n[Turn 8 ASSISTANT]\ne182\tYou're right to want a specialist.\ne183\tThree search approaches, in order of what I'd try:\n\nFirst, call the RAINN person back and ask for a referral.\ne184\tThey often maintain lists of therapists who specialize in online exploitation and grooming recovery.\ne185\tThis is the highest-signal source.\ne186\tSecond, Psychology Today's therapist directory has a filter for \"trauma\" and \"adolescents\" — cross-reference any local therapist who mentions \"online exploitation,\" \"grooming,\" or \"teen digital safety\" in their specialties.\ne187\tNot all good ones list it, but the ones who do usually have real experience.\ne188\tThird, ask your daughter's pediatrician for a referral.\ne189\tPediatricians often have networks of adolescent specialists and they can make a call on your behalf rather than you cold-calling.\ne190\tOne thing to not do: don't go with the first therapist you find just because you need this to start immediately.\ne191\tA mismatched therapist at this moment can set her against therapy for years.\ne192\tTake two weeks to find someone who does this specifically.\ne193\tIn the meantime, the RAINN person can probably do one or two phone sessions as a bridge.\ne194\tAlso — and this is important — don't tell her you're finding a therapist yet.\ne195\tThe conversation about therapy needs to happen as a joint decision with her, not as a parental edict about \"fixing you.\"\ne196\tOnce you've found someone, you present the option to her, explain why this specific therapist, and let her have some say in whether to try one session.\ne197\tIf she senses she's being \"taken\" to therapy, the therapist won't help.",
